okay so the topic of this video will be  viruses and the lytic cycle so let's go  ahead and get started so first of all  let's begin with what is a virus you  know when you look at the definition of  a virus it's pretty vague biological  particle composed of nucleic acid and  protein one of the more commonly known  facts of a virus is that they're  parasites but they're little different  than parasites that we typically think  of such as a tapeworm  viruses are intracellular parasites  meaning that they actually reside inside  of the cell of eye of a host and that's  what this video is going to focus on in  just a little bit well when we look at  this diagram we see the six kingdoms of  life plants animals fungus Protista and  then there's two bacterial kingdoms so  if something is alive on earth it's  classified in one of these six kingdoms  here so where do viruses fit in you know  the answer is nowhere viruses are not  classified as a living organism well  let's focus on that a little more right  now what I want to look at is you know  some of the characteristics that viruses  lack that really prevent them from being  classified as a living organism yet they  actually do possess some characteristics  in common with life well let's go ahead  and look at the no category on this  table here viruses are not considered  alive for a few reasons number one  they're not made of cells or organelles  they cannot reproduce on their own  without a host so here's a cell going  through mitosis you know viruses don't  do this viruses do not metabolize energy  what that means is they're not they're  like this caterpillar here they're not  herbivores like these wolves hunting  they're not carnivores  like this algae here they're they're not  autotrophs they don't feed they don't  take in energy  and they don't really perform any  cellular processes on their own so the  molecule of ATP right here should remind  you of the process called cellular  respiration well viruses don't produce  ATP and they don't perform cellular  respiration viruses do not do  photosynthesis viruses do not do DNA  replication however replication will be  an important topic in a little bit  viruses themselves do not do  transcription viruses themselves do not  do translation they don't possess the  organelles they don't possess the  enzymes they don't possess the machinery  in order to do these processes they  don't actively transport molecules into  and out of themselves so what about the  other side viruses do actually possess a  few characteristics in common with life  for instance viruses are able to  replicate they're able to make more of  themselves we're going to talk about the  lytic cycle in just a moment viruses  have nucleic acid viruses are are will  contain either DNA or RNA as their  genetic material but they do possess  nucleic acid as does every other form of  life on earth viruses are able to adapt  to their surroundings the picture here  shows some vials of of AZT which is an  HIV medication and HIV is actually the  virus HIV is adapting to the AZT  medication and therefore it's it's not  working the medication is not as  effective as it once was because the HIV  virus has adapted viruses have  organization to them they have parts  that do functions and we'll get into  some of these in just a moment  so when we look at the basic structure  of a virus I want to mention that the  shape and the size of viruses differ  from one another now they all do possess  a few characteristics in common for  instance you look at the three drawings  here and in each you'll see that the  three viruses all have what's called a  capsid and outer coating an outer layer  made up of protein and like the notes  say the protein coat to there or the  capsid surrounds nucleic acid well that  tells me the second thing that all  viruses have which is nucleic acid now  the three pictures show the viruses with  DNA inside and some viruses possess RNA  so it really does depend upon the virus  the virus on the Left labeled  bacteriophage has what almost looks like  creepy crawly legs on it for walking and  crawling around these are called tail  fibers and that's not what their  function is these tail fibers are used  for anchoring and attaching onto their  host cell so do they actually look do  these bacteria phages actually look like  this the answer is yes they actually do  now not all viruses look like this  bacteriophage right here but some of  them you can see are kind of got a  unique looking so let's start the  transition into how viruses actually  replicate you know here's a picture of a  bacteriophage and just mention these a  moment ago bacteria phages are viruses  but what's unique about them is they  actually infect bacteria so it's kind of  interesting that bacteria can even be  infected with viruses now we often will  study bacteria phages because how they  replicate is fairly similar to how  animal viruses that may get us sick for  instance also replicate so we can learn  a lot and apply it to human use now when  it comes to viral replication there's  really two two cycles to replication  pathways one is called the lytic cycle  and one  is called the lysogenic cycle the rest  of this video is really going to focus  on the lytic cycle I have a different  video if you'd like to learn more about  the lysogenic cycle so let's go ahead  and dive into the lytic cycle well we  can break the lytic cycle into some  stages and the first one is called  attachment like the name implies the  virus will attach and land onto a host  the cell membrane of a host and the  picture shows here's our host cell and  here comes a virus that has just  attached notice how there are these  little little objects on the outside of  the cell called receptors and what the  receptor will do is actually kind of  catch the virus and so the virus has  these little bumps on it made of protein  and with if the virus matches the  receptor then attachment will be  successful it's kind of like the lock  and key analogy here the virus acts as a  key the receptor acts as a lock and if  the virus is able to match the receptor  the cell not knowing anything is wrong  will draw the virus into itself and  that'll take us to step two step two is  call an entry and like the name implies  the virus will enter the host cell the  DNA or the RNA the nucleic acid will  enter the host now there's a couple ways  that this might happen  in some situations the virus will  literally inject the DNA or RNA into a  host cell that's what bacteria phages do  another way that the nucleic acid of the  virus can enter the cell is the entire  virus will ultimately be swallowed by  the cell and that's what this animation  is going to follow notice how a little  pocket has begun to form the cell is  actively swallowing the virus and taking  it into itself as the process continues  notice how the virus is drawn deeper  into the cell  and ultimately there you go the cell has  swallowed in swallowed up the the virus  and taking it inside of itself now what  happens is enzymes from the virus will  actually dissolve the outer capsid the  outer protein coat thus leaving behind  the nucleic acid of the virus so this  will now bring us the stage three called  replication and so what happens is the  virus DNA that you see will perform the  process called DNA replication to make  more viral DNA and that happens by the  viral DNA entering the nucleus of the  host cell and now we're going to see the  process of DNA replication the process  where DNA is used to make copies of more  DNA the reason the virus needs to go  into the nucleus is because that's where  the enzyme DNA polymerase is found and  so this enzyme DNA polymerase will  direct DNA replication notice what's  happening more DNA of the virus is being  copied and created well what happens  next is the virus DNA and in just a  moment the RNA that will create will be  used to make proteins that belong to the  virus well how does this occur this  occurs by the process of transcription  and translation if you forgotten what  transcription is transcription is the  process of creating RNA inside the  nucleus there's an enzyme called RNA  polymerase that will direct the process  of transcription notice what just  happened see a single strand of RNA was  just created and sent to a ribosome now  that the RNA of the virus is at a  ribosome the process called translation  can begin translation is the process of  creating proteins the difference being  the cell will actually be creating  proteins that belong to the virus  this translation process is performed by  ribosomes that's the job of a ribosome  to build proteins the ribosome doesn't  really realize that it's building  proteins for a virus so the ribosome  will read the viral RNA and begin to  build proteins that belong to the virus  and that's what the capsid of a virus is  made from the capsid of a virus is made  from proteins so the cells ribosomes are  actually building parts of future  viruses and now we come to the fourth  stage called assembly what happens is  the new viruses that have been created  start to self assemble notice how the  DNA comes together with the capsid and  when this occurs now we have brand-new  viruses that were just assembled inside  the cytoplasm of the host and so now we  come to the final stage called release  and what happens is focus on the  ribosome that you see in the picture  remember the ribosome is building  proteins that belong to the virus  another protein that the ribosomes  create that belongs to the virus are  enzymes and I've kind of drawn the  enzyme to be in the shape of scissors to  illustrate what these enzymes will do to  the cell so here's the ribosome making  enzymes enzymes that are ultimately  going to cause the host cell to lyse or  to burst and this is where the named  lytic come or lytic comes from it comes  from the word lyse which means to burst  so these enzymes that were just created  are going to pop little holes in the  cell membrane and ultimately that's  going to cause the cell to burst and the  viruses to be released  now once the viruses are released the  cycles simply going to repeat itself the  viruses are going to spread let's go  follow one of those viruses  so the viruses have spread and now  notice what's happened one of the new  viruses that just created has found  another hosts to infect to repeat the  cycle  and so that's kind of the the trickery  of the lytic cycle right there is that  even though the virus doesn't possess  any of the machinery to replicate and  make more of itself it will actually  hijack and take over a Cell which which  does possess the machinery to replicate  the virus so there you go I hope this  video was helpful if you're in my  biology class pause the video try to  answer these questions on a separate  sheet of paper I'd be happy to check  your answers before class or after class  thanks for watching
 

